LevelMinded @PROJECT_VERSION@
============================

Optimized 3D map design tool.

Run LevelMinded.exe. No installation needed; nothing else is required in this folder
(the mods folder is optional).

Requirements
------------
- Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit
- A GPU with Direct3D 11 support
- @LM_CPU_REQUIREMENT@

Viewport controls
-----------------
Alt + left drag        Orbit
Middle drag            Pan
Alt + right drag       Zoom (also: mouse wheel)
Right drag + W/A/S/D   Fly (Q/E down/up, Shift faster, wheel sets fly speed)
F / Home               Frame selection / reset view
Numpad 1 / 3 / 7       Front / right / top view (Ctrl: opposite side)
Left click             Select (Ctrl: toggle, Shift: add)
W / E / R              Move / rotate / scale the selection with the gizmo (Ctrl snaps, Esc cancels)
Delete                 Delete the selection
Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y        Undo / redo (last 100 steps; Ctrl+Shift+Z also redoes)
F1                     Show / hide the stats overlay

Panels
------
Hierarchy (left)  Click a group to select everything inside it; the eye icon hides a group and its
                  contents; drag rows to reorder or move them into groups; double-click or F2 renames.
Assets (right)    Click an item to place it at the origin, inside the active Hierarchy group
                  (shown at the top of the panel).

Maps
----
File > Load (Ctrl+O), Save (Ctrl+S), Save As (Ctrl+Shift+S). Maps are .lmmap files (readable JSON).
They keep the whole hierarchy, transforms, visibility, imported models (by file path) and the camera.
A * in the title bar means unsaved changes; you are asked before they would be lost.

Mods
----
Lua mods in the "mods" folder (.lua files, or folders with a main.lua) load at startup. The Mods panel
shows them, their controls and logs; the Mods menu has their commands and "Reload Mods". Mods are
sandboxed (no files or programs) and everything they change can be undone. mods\hello_world.lua is a
sample. Writing mods: https://github.com/KurosaCorp/Level-Minded-V2/blob/main/docs/MODDING.md

Importing models
----------------
Drag .gltf, .glb or .obj files onto the window, or use File > Import Asset (Ctrl+I).
Each model becomes a tile in the Assets panel; click it to place the model. Models keep their
authored size (1 unit = 1 meter) and are turned from Y-up to Z-up. Textures are not imported.

Command line (testing)
----------------------
--stress N         Add N items to the sample level
--bench N          Run the built-in benchmark (N frames per phase), write bench.txt, exit
--bench-out PATH   Benchmark report location
--import PATH      Import a model into the Assets panel at startup (repeatable)
--map PATH         Load a map at startup
--mods PATH        Load mods from this folder

